Differentiate with respect to $E$. Both the limits and the integrand depend on $E$, so we need the rule for differentiating such an integral (the Leibniz rule):
\begin{equation}
\frac{\dd}{\dd E}\int_{a(E)}^{b(E)}g(x,E)\,\dd x=g(b,E)\,b'(E)-g(a,E)\,a'(E)+\int_{a}^{b}\frac{\partial g}{\partial E}\,\dd x .
\label{eq:hilbert-polya-leibniz}
\end{equation}
Here is where it comes from. Write $F(a,b,E)=\int_a^b g(x,E)\,\dd x$.
\begin{itemize}
\item By the fundamental theorem of calculus, $\partial F/\partial b=g(b,E)$ and $\partial F/\partial a=-g(a,E)$.
\item Differentiating under the integral sign gives $\partial F/\partial E=\int_a^b(\partial g/\partial E)\,\dd x$. We take this step on trust; it holds for the integrands met here and is stated in \cite{rhb}.
\item The chain rule for $F(a(E),b(E),E)$ adds the three contributions, which gives Eq.~\eqref{eq:hilbert-polya-leibniz}.
\end{itemize}
Apply the rule with $g=\sqrt{E-V(x)}$, $a=x_1(E)$ and $b=x_2(E)$. The integrand vanishes at both limits ($V=E$ there), so the two boundary terms are zero and only the derivative of the integrand survives:

